  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 AM EDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\n- A round of showers and non-severe storms tonight\n\nWe will see another very nice autumn day with temperatures actually \nquite warm by mid-October standards. The low clouds on the periphery \nof the remnants of \"Isaias\" from yesterday have moved out, allowing \nfor much more sunshine, although it will become filtered with high \nclouds streaming in. 850 mb temps will warm from around 13C on \nSunday, to almost 18C which will support highs near 80.\n\nThe cold front currently north of the area will drop down into the \narea this evening and overnight as a short wave over the northern \nportion of the state pushes it overhead. Also ahead of the short \nwave and sfc low will be a 40 knot low level jet nose aimed right at \nthe area. This moves in between 00z and 06z tonight. We would expect \na good area of showers with embedded scattered storms. The storms \nare not expected to be severe as they will be elevated in nature \n(limiting wind and tornado potential) and mid level lapse rates \nneutral around 6.0 C/km and updraft strength will be limited with MU \nCAPEs a few hundred J/kg.\n\n- Additional chances of rain into Friday\n\nThe showers and storms will move out before daybreak Tuesday as the \nshort wave moves by, and the cold front sinks south of the area. The \nfront will stall out as it becomes parallel to the upper flow with \nthe cutoff low out west pumping up the ridge over the area. Much of \nTuesday should remain dry with no organized short waves expected to \nmove through.\n\nWe do see the next short wave ejecting from the western low Tuesday \nnight along the front, which will be by that time just north of the \nOhio River valley. This sets up the best chance of rain across the \nsouth, closer to the front. We will be on the cool side of the front \nby then, so thunder chances will diminish a bit. The same thing \nhappens around Thursday with the front even a tad further south. A \nbetter low is forecast to develop and move along the front, which \nwill help bring a bit better rain chances into more of the area.\n\nFast on the heels of the Thursday southern stream system, a strong \nnorthern stream wave will drop in by Friday and usher in much cooler \nair on fairly strong winds. This will bring another chance of rain \nwith the front, and could produce some brief lake effect rain \nshowers while the upper jet core briefly drops below our area.\n\nThe upper trough then moves out quickly Friday, helping to bring \nhigh pressure to the area. This will clear things out for the \nweekend.\n\n- Cooling down Thursday and beyond\n\nWe will see cooler air first arrive on Wednesday as the cold front \nsinks far enough south. Much cooler air will move in Thursday and \nFriday with more clouds and showers with the upper cold pool \noverhead. Highs may struggle to get out of the 50s. Temperatures \nwill recover a bit then starting Sunday as return flow sets up on \nthe backside of the ridge.",
